\section{Project Away Values That the Suffix Cannot Read}

An exact merge need not build conditional terms for every field
in the store. Compute which variables the suffix might read before
redefining them, including reads by the observation being checked.
For a pure scalar program, agree to preserve only future behavior
and those observations, not an assertion about a discarded prefix
trace. Assume the states are at one location, have no hidden
environment, and refer to the same input symbols. Their conditions
may overlap, so introduce one fresh shared selector for the whole
tuple; using a separate choice for each value would break correlations.

\begin{sudocode}
\Require States $(p,S)$ and $(q,T)$ at location $\ell$
\Let{L}{\operatorname{liveForSuffix}(\ell)}
\Let{b}{\operatorname{freshBoolean}()}
\To{Allow either feasible input-to-store relation.}
\Let{r}{(b\land p)\lor(\neg b\land q)}
\Let{U}{\varnothing}
\ForAll{$x\in L$}
  \To{All live values use the same selector.}
  \Let{U[x]}{\operatorname{ite}(b,S[x],T[x])}
\EndFor
\State \Return $(r,U)$
\end{sudocode}

Suppose two incoming stores have $(x,y,\mathit{tmp})=(2,9,30)$
and $(5,1,70)$, both feasible, and the suffix overwrites
$\mathit{tmp}$ before using it. It returns $x+y$.
The two selector values give $11$ and $6$; a query for $3$
is unsatisfiable. Independent selectors would admit $x=2$
and $y=1$ from different histories and falsely return $3$.
Removing the temporary avoids creating its conditional term
without losing any return. If the test objective also records
the old temporary at the join, it is live for that objective
and the projection must keep it.

The invariant is equality with the projected union of the
incoming relations once the fresh selector is hidden. Picking
either selector value gives only stores from its matching
relation, and every feasible incoming store has a selector
that represents it. With shared terms the join itself builds
at most one conditional node per live variable. Formula
solving can still dominate the cost, and a backward liveness
analysis that overlooks exception or observation uses would
make the projection unsound.

\section{Subsumption, Scheduling, and the Budget}

For a reachability objective, states at the same location can sometimes be compared instead of merged. Discarding a new state is sound when its concrete possibilities are included in those of a retained state that will be fully explored for that objective. With identical symbolic stores this may reduce to implication between path conditions: prove the conjunction of the new condition and the negation of the retained one unsatisfiable. In a test campaign, however, two equivalent future states may have arrived through different coverage-relevant prefixes. Include the coverage monitor in the state comparison if those histories matter.

Specify scheduling separately from these semantic shortcuts. A worklist may prioritize uncovered branches or alternate between broad exploration and long prefixes to avoid spending the entire budget on a loop. Incremental solver contexts can reuse common guard prefixes. Priorities do not make dropped work infeasible. Keep distinct counts of merged states, subsumed states, solver timeouts, and unexplored continuations, and report the last two as incomplete coverage. A useful comparison fixes wall-clock time and records confirmed contract outcomes with and without merging, alongside the sizes of queries each strategy actually sends.
